(verb
  :name "last-pair" :std "SRFI-1"
  :library "list"
  :tier "core"
  :kind "pure"
  :signature "(last-pair lst) -> list"
  :summary "The last cons cell of a proper list, returned as is. In Motoi this will be a real cons cell, not an array-based list segment."
  :explanation "In R7RS-compatible Motoi, last-pair returns the actual final cons cell of a proper list, which is shared with the original list and whose cdr is the terminator. This differs from today's array-based implementation where (last-pair x) allocates a fresh copy and (eq? (cdr x) (cdr x)) is #f. Once cons cells land, you can use set-cdr! on the result to modify the original list, and circular lists will become possible. The `!` verbs like set-cdr! will reuse the argument's cells, destroying the original binding. If you keep using the variable you passed in after mutation, you'll read a list that has been cut, relinked or overwritten under it. On an implementation that quietly returns a fresh list instead, that bug would be invisible, which is why it went uncaught."
  :examples (
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice" :code "(last-pair (list 1 2 3))" :note "PENDING-ENGINE")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(let ((lst (list 1 2 3)))\n  (set-cdr! (last-pair lst) (list 4))\n  (list lst (last-pair lst)))" :note "PENDING-ENGINE")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "expert" :code "(define (make-circular lst)\n  (let ((last (last-pair lst)))\n    (set-cdr! last lst)\n    lst))\n(make-circular (list 1 2 3))" :note "PENDING-ENGINE")
  )
  :caveats ("In today's array-based implementation, last-pair returns a fresh copy and eq? comparisons will fail." "Improper lists are not supported - they raise an error." "The empty list returns the empty list, not an error.")
  :drawbacks ("Requires proper cons cells which are not yet implemented in Motoi." "Cannot be used with improper lists." "The result is opaque - you can't inspect its car/cdr directly without mutation.")
  :usecases ("Getting the actual final cons cell of a list for mutation." "Creating circular lists once cons cells land." "Extending lists by modifying their last pair.")
  :related ("last" "take-right" "set-cdr!" "cons")
  :learn (:concept "Working with real cons cells" :prerequisites ("car" "cdr" "cons") :progression "Learn about proper lists and cons cells before using this verb.")
  :provenance "SRFI-1; implemented lib/std/srfi-1.js:312; documented against future cons-cell implementation"
  :row-2-audit (
    (:eng-program "(let ((lst (list 1 2 3)))\n  (define last-cell (last-pair lst))\n  (set-cdr! last-cell (list 4))\n  (display (list 'modified lst 'last-cell last-cell)))\n(newline)"
     :math-program "(define (last-pair-laws lst)\n  (and (pair? (last-pair lst))\n       (eq? (cdr (last-pair lst)) (cdr lst))))\n(display (map last-pair-laws (list (list 1 2 3) (list))))\n(newline)"
     :cs-program "(define (test-improper)\n  (try (last-pair (cons 1 2))\n       (lambda (e) (display (error-object-message e)))))\n(test-improper)\n(newline)"
     :problem "The operator needs to get the actual final cons cell of a list to perform destructive operations on it. Candidates: last-pair (returns the cons cell), last (returns just the element), or take-right 1 (returns a fresh list)."
     :explanation "eng: last-pair gives the actual cons cell which can be modified to extend the list. math: The laws show that last-pair returns a pair eq? to the original's cdr. cs: Improper lists raise an error, as they cannot have a proper last pair."
     :meta "last-pair is the only verb that gives access to the actual cons cell structure.")
    (:eng "Destructive operations require working with real cons cells." :math "last-pair maintains the eq? relationship with the original list's cdr." :cs "Improper lists are not supported and will raise errors.")
)
  :row-3-dimension (
    (:setup "The final segment of a list accessed as a manipulable cons cell."
     :rungs (
       (:domain "list" :code "(last-pair (list 1 2 3))")
       (:domain "the caboose of a train described as a car" :code "(define train (list 'engine 'freight 'caboose))\n(last-pair train)")
       (:domain "the length-1 suffix of a sequence" :code "(define (suffix1 l) (last-pair l))\n(suffix1 (list 1 2 3))")
     )
     :explanation "In list, last-pair returns the final cons cell. Describing the caboose as a car keeps it composable with descriptions of other parts. In math, the length-1 suffix is a suffix like any other. All three share: keeping the structure so the piece stays manipulable."
     :meta "The structure transfers wherever an extremum must stay in the same type as the collection so that one pipeline handles both.")
    (:eng "Structure-preserving accessors compose; unwrapping accessors force a rewrap at every joint." :math "A cons cell is still a cons cell; the empty case is what makes it a total operation rather than a partial one." :cs "This is the same reason database queries return a rowset of one rather than a scalar: the consumer stays uniform.")
)
  :row-4-proof (
    (:eng-program "(let ((lst (list 1 2 3)))\n  (define last-cell (last-pair lst))\n  (display (list 'eq? (eq? last-cell (cdr lst)) 'car (car last-cell) 'cdr (cdr last-cell))))\n(newline)"
     :math-program "(define (last-pair-laws lst)\n  (and (pair? (last-pair lst))\n       (eq? (cdr (last-pair lst)) (cdr lst))))\n(display (map last-pair-laws (list (list 1 2 3) (list))))\n(newline)"
     :cs-program "(define (test-improper)\n  (try (last-pair (cons 1 2)) (lambda (e) (display (error-object-message e)))))\n(test-improper)\n(newline)"
     :claim "For a non-empty proper list, last-pair returns the final cons cell which is eq? to the original's cdr. For the empty list it returns the empty list. For an IMPROPER list it raises an error."
     :invariant "forall non-empty proper list l. last-pair(l) is the pair eq? to (cdr (last-pair l)); last-pair(()) = ()"
     :meta "The pattern is structure-preserving extremum access. What would falsify the claim: a result that is not eq? to the original's cdr, or a car that is not the last element.")
    (:eng "A verb whose empty case returns the same type as its non-empty case never needs a null check at the joint." :math "last-pair returns the actual cons cell structure of the list." :cs "Improper lists are not supported and will raise errors.")
)
  :row-5-emergence (
    (:eng-composition "(define (append-at-end xs y)\n  (let ((last-cell (last-pair xs)))\n    (set-cdr! last-cell (list y))\n    xs))\n(display (append-at-end (list 1 2 3) 4))\n(newline)"
     :math-composition "(define (segments xs)\n  (if (null? xs)\n      (list)\n      (list (list (car xs)) (drop-right (cdr xs) 1) (last-pair xs))))\n(display (segments (list 1 2 3 4 5)))\n(newline)"
     :cs-composition "(define (make-circular lst)\n  (let ((last-cell (last-pair lst)))\n    (set-cdr! last-cell lst)\n    lst))\n(make-circular (list 1 2 3))"
     :base "last-pair returns the final cons cell of a list."
     :emergent "eng: end-extension written destructively - in a pair-based Scheme this would be set-cdr! on the last pair, and here it is the honest way to say the same thing without mutation. math: three-way segmentation into head, middle and tail, where every piece is a list and the pieces append back to the original. cs: creating circular lists by setting the cdr of the last pair to the whole list."
     :meta "The composition uses last-pair together with drop-right to carve a list into named segments. What emerges is the segmentation family: bookends, head-middle-tail splits, and the functional replacement for last-pair mutation.")
    (:eng "Matching shapes at both ends means the reporting code has one branch, not three." :math "Head, middle and tail are a partition: they are disjoint, ordered, and their concatenation is the whole." :cs "The classic set-cdr! on last-pair trick is available in Motoi; the append form is what replaces it, and it is O(n) rather than O(1).")
)
